A||||7||Eric||Making the 88 handle well.||||1||
Z||000000||Eric||05-07-2000||07:22 PM||||You guys are probably thinking "What, the 88s don't handle well enough?"  That's right, they don't.  I need to find a way to get rid of my push.  The understeer condition is really tricky to control unlike oversteer.  It seems like to front end is unloaded to easily when power is applied.  Good shocks might help this, but I really think dropping the front end would make this car understeer less.  Can anyone offer any advise on lowering springs (not spindles cause they're too expensive)?  Are there good springs available for the 88?||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||05-07-2000||08:43 PM||||Get some really sticky tires. I know that sounds too simple but tires make the biggest difference in the way the car handles. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||05-07-2000||09:43 PM||||Yesterday I installed a 1" rear swaybar. You want to even out the under/oversteer with much less throttle? Get you one of these 1" bars. I didn't have a factory bar to compare it to, but it feels almost neutral now. The rear is actually a little slippery and I plan to get a 1 1/4" front bar to even it out. Needless to say, my car felt like a car I have never driven before... have to learn to drive it all over again.<P>Stiffer rear springs or stiffer struts could help keep the front from unloading so much.<P>And tires make all the difference. Today I ran my GT wheels with 512/60s and didn't even compare to my other wheels with 225/50ZR/16s.<p>[This message has been edited by SCCA FIERO (edited 05-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ozzy||05-07-2000||11:56 PM||||Yuppers! I can't beleive how much difference tires make to an '88! The only suspension mods I've done are front poly bushings and new shocks plus replace the LBJ's with Moog's and Rodney Dickmans pass. side rack bushing replacement. With the front end nice and tight, I bought some 15" rims and Goodyear Eagle HP's (215/60/15). They made the car track like crap and almost felt kinda unstable! I bought a parts car that had the turbo alloys and some Cenntenial 215/60/14's on 'em. I can't understand why but this car feels like a Go Cart with these tires and rims...Go Figure!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>Good thing the 15" tires and rims work well on the GT!<P>Ozzy||||reg||12
Z||000004||WisconsinGT||05-08-2000||01:29 AM||||Yeah Ozzy, tires do alot for the 88 suspension. I had 215/60/15 all around. On Friday of last week, I put Michelin Energy                                             MXV4 Plus 205/60/15's on the front. What a difference! Damn, the car rides like a million bucks now.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierosound||05-08-2000||04:02 AM||||WisconsinGT,<P>You have probably noticed it's easier to steer too!!!  Almost like power steering!!  I noticed a huge difference in steering response when I got MXV4s all around on my 87 GT.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||05-08-2000||04:11 AM||||Eibach springs, urethane bushings do wonders for the 88 suspension too!||||reg||2
Z||000008||Eric||05-08-2000||02:18 PM||||WKDFIRO, where did you get your Eibach springs?<P>I already have some super sticky tires, the problem is the way the front end unloads.  That's why I want to lower it.  I'm also planning on putting Koni's on the car and poly bushings too, but maybe just on the rear.  I have 205s in front and 225s in back so I may have too much tire in the rear for balanced handling.  I'll go with 215s next time.  I also want to move the battery to the front.<P>||||reg||1
